Obtain the vehicle's inspection history, if any, to see if there have been previous failures or recurring issues. This can provide context and help establish a pattern of maintenance or neglect.

3. Preliminary inspection.
Conduct a brief walk-around to observe any obvious signs of brake wear or damage, such as fluid leaks, uneven tire wear, or ruined brake components.

4. Documentation of pre-test conditions.
Record the following details before carrying out the brake tests:

   - Date and time of inspection;
   - Mileage of the vehicle;
   - Make, model, and year of the vehicle;
   - VIN (Vehicle Identification Number);
   - Identifying feature or defects noticed during the walk-around.

5. Conduct the brake tests.
Follow standardized procedures for brake testing, which may include:

   - Visual inspections (wear on pads and rotors, condition of hoses and lines, etc.);
   - Operation tests (pedal travel, pedal pulsation, holding power, etc.);
   - Instrumented tests (using brake testers or dynamometers to measure braking efficiency);

If any test fails, make detailed notes of exactly what happened, including:

   - Test description and procedure followed;
   - Actual results versus expected results;
   - Whether the failure is on a specific wheel or within the braking system as a whole (e.g., hydraulic failure);
   - Relevant observations such as unusual noises, odors, or behaviors.

6. Additional checks.
If time permits, perform additional checks to pinpoint the source of failure:

   - Inspect the brake fluid level and condition;
   - Check the brake drum or rotor condition for any signs of overheating or excessive wear;
   - Look for excessive play or wear in the ball joints, wheel bearings, or suspension components that could affect braking performance.

7. Photographs and sketches.
Use a camera to capture images of any visible defects or malfunctions. Sketches can also be helpful in illustrating the damage or wear particularly if the failed component is complex or obscured.

8. Consultation with repair professionals.
When the failure is outside the scope of the inspector's expertise, consider consulting with a certified mechanic or auto technician to get more insight or confirmation of the issue found.

9. Detailed report preparation.
Prepare the inspection report with the following key components:

   - Title page with the vehicle’s details, the inspection date, and inspector's information;
   - Executive summary giving a brief overview of the inspection findings and failed brake test results;
   - Detailed description of the tests conducted, including step-by-step procedures, measurements, and observations;
   - Supporting documentation: Include photographs, diagrams, and any other evidence to substantiate the results;
   - List of failed components or systems and recommendations for repair;
   - Conclusions: Summarize why the vehicle failed the inspection and the implications (e.g., unsafe operation, need for immediate repair);
   - Signature of the inspector attesting to the accuracy of the report.

10. Submit report to the relevant authority.
Depending on local regulations, submit the inspection report to the designated regulatory agency, vehicle owner, or fleet manager. The report should be clear, complete, and accurate to meet legal requirements and provide a basis for determining the necessary corrective actions.

11. Follow-ups.
Monitor the follow-up actions taken after the failed inspection, such as repairs, re-inspection, and compliance with the recommended corrective measures. Maintain records and communication regarding the follow-up procedures.

12. Secure all data and documents.
Store all inspection records securely and in compliance with privacy laws and data retention policies. Digital records should be backed up, and physical documents kept in a safe place, accessible only to those who are authorized.

By following these instructions, inspectors can ensure that failed brake tests are accurately documented, providing a crucial record for vehicle maintenance, safety, and regulatory compliance.

